# ==================================================================
# Claude Debussy - Scales and Pitch Collections
# ==================================================================
#
# Claude Debussy (1862-1918) was a French composer whose harmonic
# language broke decisively with 19th-century tonality. Rather than
# developing a single personal system (as Scriabin did), Debussy
# drew freely from multiple pitch collections, treating them as
# interchangeable colors in a connected harmonic network.
#
# His pitch world revolves around five key collections included here:
#
#   1. Whole-tone scale       - 6 notes, 1 mode,  2 transpositions
#   2. Octatonic scale        - 8 notes, 2 modes, 3 transpositions
#   3. Pentatonic             - 5 notes, 5 modes, 12 transpositions
#   4. Diatonic modes         - 7 notes, 7 modes, 12 transpositions
#      (Ionian, Dorian, Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian, Aeolian, Locrian)
#   5. Acoustic / Lydian dom. - 7 notes, 7 modes, 12 transpositions
#
# Total scale entries: 1 + 2 + 5 + 7 + 7 = 22
#
# ==================================================================
# SHARED COLLECTIONS WITH SCRIABIN
# ==================================================================
#
# Scales 1 (whole-tone) and 2 (octatonic) are shared with Scriabin.
# Scale 5 (acoustic) is also shared: in Scriabin it is the parent
# of the Prometheus/Mystic collection (the Prometheus scale = the
# acoustic scale with the perfect 5th omitted).
#
# The key differences between the two composers:
#   - Debussy's DISTINCTIVE additions: pentatonic (gamelan-derived)
#     and the systematic revival of the diatonic church modes.
#   - Debussy's octatonic use: shorter passages, fluid juxtaposition
#     with other collections (unlike Stravinsky's structural blocks).
#   - Debussy's whole-tone use: even more central and pervasive than
#     in Scriabin — so much so that Messiaen said after Debussy
#     there was "nothing else to add" to the whole-tone scale.
#
# ==================================================================
# SCALE NETWORK (Tymoczko 2004)
# ==================================================================
#
# Dmitri Tymoczko, in "Scale Networks and Debussy" (Journal of Music
# Theory, 48.2, 2004), demonstrates that these scales form a
# connected network where adjacent collections share maximum common
# tones — precisely the voice-leading relationships Debussy exploited
# to move fluidly between harmonic worlds. The acoustic scale acts
# as a mediator between whole-tone and diatonic collections, just as
# in Scriabin it mediates between whole-tone and octatonic via the
# Prometheus collection. This is a striking structural parallel
# between the two composers' harmonic languages.
#
# ==================================================================
# HISTORICAL REFERENCES BY SCALE
# ==================================================================
#
# 1. WHOLE-TONE:
#    - Messiaen: "after Debussy, there was nothing else to add"
#      (formalized it as his Mode 1 of Limited Transposition)
#    - Britannica: "some outstanding examples of whole-tone harmony
#      are Debussy's Voiles (1909) and Cloches a travers les feuilles"
#    - Gamelan link: possibly Debussy's analogue of the gamelan's
#      7-note pelog tuning (brenthugh.com/debnotes/gamelan.html)
#    - Key works: Voiles (Preludes Bk 1), La mer, Pelleas Act 4,
#      Images Bk 1, Prelude a l'apres-midi d'un faune (bb.32-36),
#      Cloches a travers les feuilles (Images Bk 2)
#
# 2. OCTATONIC:
#    - Allen Forte, "Debussy and the Octatonic" (Music Analysis, 1991)
#    - Mark DeVoto on Nuages: "arguably Debussy's boldest single
#      leap into the musical unknown"
#    - WJEC analysis notes: Debussy used octatonic in shorter passages,
#      juxtaposing it with whole-tone and diatonic material, exploiting
#      shared subsets between the different collections
#    - Key work: Nuages (Nocturnes, 1899) - cor anglais melody built
#      on B octatonic collection; bars 33-42 juxtapose whole-tone
#      and octatonic material
#
# 3. PENTATONIC:
#    - Root event: 1889 Paris Exposition Universelle. Debussy heard
#      Javanese gamelan in the Dutch pavilion and returned "again and
#      again" (Tamagawa dissertation, Univ. of Texas, 1988)
#    - Debussy's own words: "the Javanese music makes our tonic and
#      dominant seem like empty phantoms for the use of unwise infants"
#      (letter cited in W.W. Norton, The Enjoyment of Music, 10e)
#    - Debussy heard a larger ensemble again at the 1900 Exposition
#    - Pentatonic mimics gamelan's slendro tuning (5-note system);
#      whole-tone possibly analogous to 7-note pelog
#    - In Pagodes alone, Debussy uses 5-6 variants of the pentatonic
#      to evoke the subtly out-of-tune quality of slendro tuning
#    - Jeremy Day-O'Connell, "Debussy, Pentatonicism, and the Tonal
#      Tradition" (Music Theory Spectrum, 31/2, 2009) — the definitive
#      academic study
#    - Key works: Pagodes (Estampes 1903), La cathedrale engloutie,
#      Et la lune descend sur le temple qui fut (Images Bk 2), La mer
#
# 4. DIATONIC MODES:
#    - Wikipedia (Mode, music): "modality REPLACES diatonic tonality
#      in the music of Claude Debussy and Bela Bartok"
#    - Leonard Bernstein (Young People's Concerts, 1958): analyzes
#      Fetes (Nocturnes) bar by bar, showing Dorian/Lydian/Mixolydian
#      in the first 30 seconds; La cathedrale engloutie as Mixolydian
#    - Historical origin: the four authentic church modes (Dorian,
#      Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian) were codified by medieval monks
#      in the 11th century to classify Gregorian chant. Ionian (major)
#      and Aeolian (natural minor) survived into common-practice
#      tonality; the others were revived by Debussy as fresh colors
#    - Key piece/mode evidence:
#      Ionian:     Clair de lune (Db major), La fille aux cheveux de
#                  lin (Gb major), L'isle joyeuse second theme (A major)
#      Dorian:     Fetes opening dance theme; La cathedrale bb.22-27
#      Phrygian:   La cathedrale (opening, C Phrygian/Aeolian mix);
#                  La soiree dans Grenade (Iberian flavor)
#      Lydian:     L'isle joyeuse (1904) — Jim Samson (1977): "the
#                  Lydian mode functioning as an effective mediator
#                  between whole-tone and diatonic"; also in Fetes
#      Mixolydian: La cathedrale engloutie — Bernstein: "Debussy used
#                  this same Mixolydian mode" for the cathedral theme;
#                  bars 33-37 confirmed Mixolydian (Bb included);
#                  also alternating with Dorian throughout Fetes
#      Aeolian:    La cathedrale (natural minor elements); general use
#      Locrian:    WEAK attribution — included for completeness as
#                  the 7th diatonic rotation. Almost unusable as a
#                  tonal center due to diminished tonic triad.
#                  No strong documented use in Debussy.
#
# 5. ACOUSTIC / LYDIAN DOMINANT:
#    - Wikipedia (Acoustic scale): "appears very frequently in the
#      works of Claude Debussy" (citing Tymoczko 2004)
#    - Tymoczko (2004, p.258): L'isle joyeuse "involves a central
#      acoustic scale" as its primary organizational collection
#    - WJEC Nuages analysis: "G acoustic scale used from bar 21 as
#      a compromise between the octatonic and whole-tone scales"
#    - The acoustic scale = Mixolydian with raised 4th. Its 7 modes
#      are identical to the 7 modes of ascending melodic minor.
#
# ==================================================================
# REFERENCES
# ==================================================================
#
#   - Tymoczko, D. (2004). Scale Networks and Debussy.
#     Journal of Music Theory, 48(2), 219-294.
#     https://dmitri.mycpanel.princeton.edu/debussy.pdf
#   - Forte, A. (1991). Debussy and the Octatonic.
#     Music Analysis, 10(1-2), 125-169.
#   - Day-O'Connell, J. (2009). Debussy, Pentatonicism, and the
#     Tonal Tradition. Music Theory Spectrum, 31(2), 225-261.
#   - Tamagawa, K. (1988). Echoes from the East: The Javanese
#     Gamelan and its Influence on the Music of Claude Debussy.
#     Dissertation, University of Texas, Austin.
#   - Bernstein, L. (1958). What Is a Mode? Young People's Concerts.
#     https://leonardbernstein.com/lectures/television-scripts/
#     young-peoples-concerts/what-is-a-mode
#   - Hugh, B. (2001). Debussy and the Gamelan.
#     https://brenthugh.com/debnotes/gamelan.html
#   - Wikipedia: Whole-tone scale, Octatonic scale, Acoustic scale,
#     Mode (music), L'isle joyeuse
#   - Companion file: Alexander Scriabin - Scales and Pitch Collections
#
# File author: Giorgio Robino
# Format: REAPER .reascale
# ==================================================================
#
# Interval notation reminder:
#   The 12-digit sequence maps semitones 0..11 from the root (C).
#   Non-zero digits = scale degrees (1=root, 2..N=successive degrees).
#   Zero = note not in the scale.
#
# ==================================================================


# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. WHOLE-TONE SCALE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Intervals: 2-2-2-2-2-2  |  6 notes  |  2 transpositions  |  1 mode
#
# Shared with Scriabin, but even more central to Debussy's identity.
# Messiaen (who codified it as his Mode 1 of Limited Transposition)
# stated that after Debussy's exploration there was "nothing else to
# add." Its maximal symmetry — all rotations produce the same
# interval pattern — means there is genuinely only 1 mode.
#
# A game-changing feature for Debussy: without a leading tone or
# dominant-tonic pull, the scale creates a blurred, hovering effect
# that dissolves functional tonality. All triads built from it are
# augmented, reinforcing harmonic ambiguity.
#
# Voiles (Preludes Bk 1, 1909) is composed almost entirely within
# one whole-tone scale, with a brief contrasting pentatonic section.
# Other documented uses: Cloches a travers les feuilles (Images
# Bk 2, 1907), La mer, Jeux, Images pour piano No.1, Pelleas
# et Melisande Act 4 sc.2, Prelude a l'apres-midi d'un faune
# (bars 32-33 and 35-36).
#
# Notes from C: C  D  E  F#  Ab  Bb

2 "Debussy - Whole-tone"

0 "Whole-tone  2-2-2-2-2-2"              102030405060

-2


# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. OCTATONIC SCALE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Intervals: 1-2-1-2-1-2-1-2 (and rotation)
# 8 notes  |  3 transpositions  |  2 modes
#
# Shared with Scriabin. This is the "diminished scale," also
# Messiaen's Mode 2. Its 3-fold symmetry (repeats every minor third)
# limits it to 3 distinct transpositions.
#
# Debussy's use differs importantly from Stravinsky's: rather than
# building large structural blocks in the octatonic, Debussy used
# it in shorter passages, juxtaposing it fluidly with whole-tone
# and diatonic material and exploiting the shared subsets between
# collections (the voice-leading network described by Tymoczko 2004).
#
# The clearest documented use is in Nuages (Nocturnes, 1899).
# Allen Forte (Music Analysis, 1991) identifies the cor anglais
# melody (bars 5-8) as octatonic. Mark DeVoto calls Nuages
# "arguably Debussy's boldest single leap into the musical unknown,"
# defining "a kind of tonality never heard before, based on the
# centricity of a diminished tonic triad B-D-F natural."
# Bars 33-42 of the same piece directly juxtapose whole-tone and
# octatonic material — a textbook example of scale-network thinking.

2 "Debussy - Octatonic"

# Mode 1: half-whole  1-2-1-2-1-2-1-2
# Notes: C  C#  Eb  E  F#  G  A  Bb
0 "Octatonic  1-2-1-2-1-2-1-2  (half-whole)"   110230450660

-1

# Mode 2: whole-half  2-1-2-1-2-1-2-1
# Notes: C  D  Eb  F  F#  Ab  A  B
0 "Octatonic  2-1-2-1-2-1-2-1  (whole-half)"   102204405607

-2


# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. PENTATONIC SCALE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Intervals: 2-2-3-2-3 (major pentatonic base)
# 5 notes  |  12 transpositions  |  5 modes
#
# The most distinctly Debussy addition vs. the Scriabin palette.
#
# PRIMARY SOURCE — 1889 Paris Exposition Universelle:
# Debussy, then 27, heard Javanese gamelan music in the Dutch
# pavilion and returned "again and again" (Tamagawa, 1988).
# His own words in a letter: "the Javanese music makes our tonic
# and dominant seem like empty phantoms for the use of unwise
# infants" (cited in W.W. Norton, The Enjoyment of Music, 10e).
# He encountered a fuller gamelan ensemble again at the 1900
# Paris Exposition.
#
# PENTATONIC AND THE GAMELAN SLENDRO TUNING:
# The pentatonic scale approximates gamelan's slendro tuning
# (a 5-note system, slightly different from equal-tempered
# pentatonic). In Pagodes alone, Debussy uses 5-6 different
# versions of the pentatonic scale, partly to evoke the subtly
# out-of-tune quality of slendro (Day-O'Connell, 2009).
#
# Key works: Pagodes (Estampes, 1903) — most thoroughgoing use;
# La cathedrale engloutie (pentatonic + modal mix);
# Et la lune descend sur le temple qui fut (Images Bk 2);
# La mer (1905); Bells Through the Leaves (Images Bk 2).
#
# Being an asymmetric 5-note scale, all 5 rotations produce
# genuinely distinct interval sequences and sonic characters.
# Names below follow standard ethnomusicological terminology.
#
# NOTE ON ATTRIBUTION: The major pentatonic (base) and minor
# pentatonic (Rot.4) are the best-documented in Debussy. The
# intermediate rotations (Egyptian, Man Gong, Ritusen/Yo) are
# included as members of the same 5-mode family, though their
# direct attribution in Debussy is less specific.

2 "Debussy - Pentatonic"

# Base: 2-2-3-2-3  [Major pentatonic]
# Notes: C  D  E  G  A
# The primary pentatonic form in Debussy. Associated with the
# gamelan slendro tuning and the archaic/pastoral quality Debussy
# sought in pieces like Pagodes. No semitones = no tonal pull,
# creating a floating, consonant spaciousness.
0 "Pentatonic  2-2-3-2-3  (Major)"               102030040500

-1

# Rotation 1: 2-3-2-3-2  [Egyptian / Suspended pentatonic]
# Notes: C  D  F  G  Bb
# Starting on the 2nd degree of the major pentatonic. Also called
# the "suspended" pentatonic in jazz. Adds a more ambiguous,
# unresolved quality compared to the major pentatonic.
0 "Pentatonic Rot.1  2-3-2-3-2  (Egyptian)"       102003040050

-1

# Rotation 2: 3-2-3-2-2  [Man Gong / Minor blues pentatonic]
# Notes: C  Eb  F  Ab  Bb
# Starting on the 3rd degree of the major pentatonic. The darkest
# of the five rotations. Less directly documented in Debussy but
# present as part of the complete pentatonic color family.
0 "Pentatonic Rot.2  3-2-3-2-2  (Man Gong)"       100203004050

-1

# Rotation 3: 2-3-2-2-3  [Ritusen / Yo pentatonic]
# Notes: C  D  F  G  A
# Starting on the 4th degree of the major pentatonic. Used in
# Japanese traditional music (the "yo" scale). Plausibly within
# Debussy's palette alongside the major pentatonic given his
# documented interest in East Asian music.
0 "Pentatonic Rot.3  2-3-2-2-3  (Ritusen / Yo)"   102003040500

-1

# Rotation 4: 3-2-2-3-2  [Minor pentatonic]
# Notes: C  Eb  F  G  Bb
# Starting on the 5th degree of the major pentatonic. The relative
# minor pentatonic. Present in Debussy as a complement to the major
# pentatonic in modal and archaic passages.
0 "Pentatonic Rot.4  3-2-2-3-2  (Minor)"          100203040050

