# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. DIATONIC MODES
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Intervals: from 2-2-1-2-2-2-1 (Ionian base) with 6 rotations
# 7 notes  |  12 transpositions  |  7 modes
#
# One of Debussy's most documented tools for escaping common-
# practice tonality. Wikipedia (Mode, music) states bluntly that
# "modality REPLACES diatonic tonality in the music of Claude
# Debussy and Bela Bartok."
#
# HISTORICAL ORIGIN OF THE MODES:
# The four "authentic" medieval church modes — Dorian, Phrygian,
# Lydian, Mixolydian — were codified around the 11th century by
# monks classifying Gregorian chant. The system expanded to 7 modes
# in the Renaissance (adding Ionian, Aeolian, Locrian). By Bach's
# time, all but Ionian (major) and Aeolian (natural minor) had
# disappeared from art music. Debussy revived the remaining five
# as fresh expressive colors, achieving archaic, floating, or exotic
# qualities without resorting to atonality.
#
# Bernstein (Young People's Concerts, 1958) famously analyzed Fetes
# (Nocturnes): "in the first half-minute of the piece, Debussy has
# already used three different modes, none of them major or minor:
# Dorian, Lydian, and Mixolydian."
#
# NOTE ON IONIAN AND AEOLIAN:
# These are the familiar major and natural minor scales, included
# here for completeness as members of the same 7-mode diatonic
# family. They are also present in REAPER's default scale library.
#
# NOTE ON LOCRIAN:
# The 7th rotation is included for theoretical completeness.
# Its diminished tonic triad (lowered 2nd AND lowered 5th above
# the root) makes it almost impossible to establish as a stable
# tonal center in any style. Debussy attribution is WEAK — if
# present at all, only in passing transitional moments.
#
# KEY PIECE EVIDENCE BY MODE:
#   Ionian:     Clair de lune (Db major), La fille aux cheveux de
#               lin (Gb major), L'isle joyeuse second theme (A major)
#   Dorian:     Fetes (Nocturnes) opening dance theme; La cathedrale
#               engloutie bars 22-27 (confirmed in score analysis)
#   Phrygian:   La cathedrale engloutie opening (C Phrygian/Aeolian);
#               La soiree dans Grenade (Iberian Phrygian flavor)
#   Lydian:     L'isle joyeuse (1904) — Jim Samson (1977): "the
#               Lydian mode functioning as an effective mediator
#               between whole-tone and diatonic"; also in Fetes
#   Mixolydian: La cathedrale engloutie — Bernstein: "Debussy used
#               this same Mixolydian mode" for the cathedral theme;
#               bars 33-37 confirmed Mixolydian (Bb causes it);
#               alternating with Dorian throughout Fetes
#   Aeolian:    La cathedrale (natural minor elements alongside
#               Phrygian); general presence in modal passages
#   Locrian:    WEAK — 7th diatonic rotation, included for
#               completeness only

2 "Debussy - Diatonic Modes"

# Base: 2-2-1-2-2-2-1  [Ionian / Major scale]
# Notes: C  D  E  F  G  A  B
# The familiar major scale. Debussy used it — especially for lyrical
# second themes and moments of tonal resolution — but constantly
# escaped from it into other modes. Its presence makes the modal
# departures more vivid by contrast.
# Key works: Clair de lune, La fille aux cheveux de lin,
# L'isle joyeuse (second theme in pure A major).
0 "Diatonic  2-2-1-2-2-2-1  (Ionian / Major)"         102034050607

-1

# Rotation 1: 2-1-2-2-2-1-2  [Dorian]
# Notes: C  D  Eb  F  G  A  Bb
# A minor mode, but with a characteristic raised 6th (major 6th
# above root) that lifts it above natural minor, giving a quality
# often described as "noble" or "archaic."
# Key works: Fetes (Nocturnes) opening dance theme in Dorian;
# La cathedrale engloutie bars 22-27.
0 "Diatonic Rot.1  2-1-2-2-2-1-2  (Dorian)"           102304050670

-1

# Rotation 2: 1-2-2-2-1-2-2  [Phrygian]
# Notes: C  Db  Eb  F  G  Ab  Bb
# Defined by its half-step above the root (lowered 2nd degree),
# giving a Spanish, dark, or archaic quality. Used by Debussy for
# cathedral and Iberian imagery. La cathedrale's opening is built
# on a C Phrygian/Aeolian palette; La soiree dans Grenade (Estampes)
# draws on the Phrygian's Spanish-Moorish flavor.
0 "Diatonic Rot.2  1-2-2-2-1-2-2  (Phrygian)"         120304056070

-1

# Rotation 3: 2-2-2-1-2-2-1  [Lydian]
# Notes: C  D  E  F#  G  A  B
# A major mode with a raised 4th (tritone above root), giving a
# dreamy, luminous, slightly unstable quality — distinct from plain
# major by that one sharpened degree.
# Key work: L'isle joyeuse (1904). Jim Samson (1977): "the central
# relationship in the work is between material based on the whole-
# tone scale, the Lydian mode and the diatonic scale, the Lydian
# mode functioning as an effective mediator between the other two."
# This mediating role mirrors the acoustic scale's function in
# Tymoczko's scale network.
0 "Diatonic Rot.3  2-2-2-1-2-2-1  (Lydian)"           102030450607

-1

# Rotation 4: 2-2-1-2-2-1-2  [Mixolydian]
# Notes: C  D  E  F  G  A  Bb
# A major mode with a lowered 7th (no leading tone). The absence of
# the leading tone removes harmonic direction, creating a grand,
# archaic, or modal quality — perfect for Debussy's cathedral imagery.
# Key works: La cathedrale engloutie — Bernstein: "Debussy, when he
# wanted to suggest a cathedral rising out of the sea, used this same
# Mixolydian mode." Confirmed by score analysis (bars 33-37, Bb
# switches passage to C Mixolydian). Also prominent throughout Fetes.
0 "Diatonic Rot.4  2-2-1-2-2-1-2  (Mixolydian)"       102034050670

-1

# Rotation 5: 2-1-2-2-1-2-2  [Aeolian / Natural minor]
# Notes: C  D  Eb  F  G  Ab  Bb
# The natural minor scale. Present throughout Debussy alongside
# Phrygian in passages of archaic or somber character.
# La cathedrale engloutie's opening section contains Phrygian and
# Aeolian elements intertwined. Also present in REAPER's default
# scale library.
0 "Diatonic Rot.5  2-1-2-2-1-2-2  (Aeolian / Natural minor)"  102304056070

-1

# Rotation 6: 1-2-2-1-2-2-2  [Locrian]
# Notes: C  Db  Eb  F  Gb  Ab  Bb
# The 7th and final diatonic rotation. Defined by a diminished
# tonic triad (lowered 2nd AND lowered 5th), making it practically
# unusable as a stable tonal center in tonal or modal music.
# ATTRIBUTION CAVEAT: No strong documented use in Debussy.
# Included strictly for theoretical completeness — as the inevitable
# 7th member of the diatonic mode family. Any Locrian-like passages
# in Debussy are likely transitional or incidental, not structural.
0 "Diatonic Rot.6  1-2-2-1-2-2-2  (Locrian)"          120304506070

-2


# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. ACOUSTIC SCALE / LYDIAN DOMINANT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Intervals: 2-2-2-1-2-1-2  |  7 notes  |  12 transpositions  |  7 modes
#
# Also known as: Overtone scale, Lydian b7, Mixolydian #4.
# From C: C D E F# G A Bb  (degrees: 1-2-3-#4-5-6-b7)
#
# Shared with Scriabin (where it is the "parent scale" of the
# Prometheus/Mystic collection: Prometheus = Acoustic minus the
# perfect 5th). Wikipedia (Acoustic scale) states it "appears very
# frequently in the works of Claude Debussy" (citing Tymoczko 2004).
#
# STRUCTURAL ROLE IN DEBUSSY'S SCALE NETWORK:
# Tymoczko (2004) shows the acoustic scale as a critical node
# between the whole-tone and diatonic collections: it shares notes
# C D E F# A Bb with the whole-tone scale and connects smoothly
# to diatonic collections through minimal voice leading. It is
# essentially a Mixolydian scale with a raised 4th (F# instead of F).
# This mediating function explains why it appears in pieces that
# navigate between worlds, like L'isle joyeuse and Nuages.
#
# Key piece evidence:
#   L'isle joyeuse (1904): Tymoczko (2004, p.258) describes it as
#   "involving a central acoustic scale" as its primary collection.
#   Nuages (Nocturnes, 1899): The G acoustic scale appears from bar
#   21, described by the WJEC analysis as "a compromise between the
#   octatonic and whole-tone scales" — exactly its network role.
#
# TERM ETYMOLOGY:
# The term "acoustic scale" was coined by Erno Lendvai (1955) in
# his analysis of Bela Bartok. The name refers to the scale's
# resemblance to the 8th-14th partials of the harmonic series.
# In jazz it was formalized by George Russell (1953) as the
# "Lydian dominant" in his Lydian Chromatic Concept.
#
# EQUIVALENCE TO THE MELODIC MINOR MODES:
# The 7 rotations of this scale are IDENTICAL to the complete set
# of 7 modes of the ascending melodic minor scale:
#
#   Base   2-2-2-1-2-1-2  =  Mode 4  Lydian dominant
#   Rot.1  2-2-1-2-1-2-2  =  Mode 5  Mixolydian b6
#   Rot.2  2-1-2-1-2-2-2  =  Mode 6  Locrian #2
#   Rot.3  1-2-1-2-2-2-2  =  Mode 7  Altered / Super Locrian
#   Rot.4  2-1-2-2-2-2-1  =  Mode 1  Melodic minor (ascending)
#   Rot.5  1-2-2-2-2-1-2  =  Mode 2  Dorian b2
#   Rot.6  2-2-2-2-1-2-1  =  Mode 3  Lydian augmented
#
# Being asymmetric and 7-note, all 6 rotations are genuinely
# distinct — the richest mode family in this collection.

2 "Debussy - Acoustic / Lydian Dominant"

# Base: 2-2-2-1-2-1-2  [Melodic minor Mode 4: Lydian dominant]
# Notes: C  D  E  F#  G  A  Bb
# The "central acoustic scale" of L'isle joyeuse (Tymoczko 2004).
# Mediates between whole-tone (shares C D E F# A Bb) and diatonic.
# = Mixolydian with raised 4th (F# instead of F).
